hi I'm dr. Scarlett Howard I'm a  postdoctoral research fellow at the  University of Toulouse I'm a scientist  working on the cognitive abilities of  honeybees I have been specializing in  the past for the past four years on the  ability of bees to learn numbers and use  number to solve problem learn complex  numerical concepts and that sort of  thing so I've been able to show that  honeybees can add and subtract and they  can value numbers along a number line  to train bees to add and subtract we  train them to fly into a why misshaped  apparatus when they flew in davis or a  number it could have been three dots for  example then if those dots were blue the  B learned to add if they were yellow the  be learned to subtract by one each time  so when the bee flew in if she saw three  blue dots she'd then fly into another  chamber where she see two options and if  that was one more then the number she'd  seen before so if we saw three and it  was blue she should go to four if she  saw three and it was yellow she should  go to two and so over the course of a  hundred learning events the bee got a  reward of sugar water for choosing  correctly and she tasted a bitter  substance when she chose incorrectly as  she came back from hive over and over  again she slowed learnt addition and  subtraction over a hundred trials  the honeybee has a brain which is has  less than a million neurons compared to  our brains which have about 100 billion  and so being able to look at the brain  which is a relatively small and simple  brain gives us a lot of insight into  things such as how to build better  artificial intelligence how to make  computer processing more powerful and it  can even tell us a lot about our own  evolution and the ability of our own  brains  studying the numerical abilities of  Bay's does tell us a lot about human  mathematical ability today and that's  because while we share a common ancestor  over 600 million years ago with Bayes  seeing that both humans and bees have  similar levels of numerical ability and  can do similar tasks while we're a lot  more sophisticated than the Bayes and  our abilities it means that we probably  have a common ancestor 600 million years  ago that had these numerical processing  skills and I find that really  interesting
 

